extends RefCounted

# Original scenario introductions. Mission names and commissions come from the
# original scenario facts; objectives and live quantities come from the simulation.
const BRIEFS := [
	"At Nubt the first families arrive on foot. Mark out a short street and leave room beside it for homes. Once the hunters are bringing meat back from the desert, give a water carrier a route past the doors of the village.",
	"The gold is in the rock, but the treasury is at Thinis. Choose a palace site with groundwater and give the mine a road to its gates. A town where the carts run steadily can afford a temple to Bast and a booth for the jugglers.",
	"At Perwadjyt the flood does part of the work. The rest falls to the people in the work camps. Set a granary within reach of the fig fields, then make room for the potters and the buyers who will supply the town.",
	"Nekhen has outgrown its village streets. A physician, a musician and a cart of beer each need a route through the same neighborhoods. Ten apartment households will tell you whether those streets work.",
	"A teacher is waiting for papyrus at Men-nefer. Bring the reeds to a workshop and the finished rolls to a scribal school. The same workshops can pay for imported bricks when the royal masons mark out their tomb.",
	"The road to Timna must earn its keep. Keep an eye on the wages going out of the treasury and the cargo coming in. Pharaoh will judge the settlement by the households that stay and the money left after they have been paid.",
	"At Behdet the surveyors have marked a tomb larger than the one at Men-nefer. Before laying the first course, follow a brick from its source to the proposed building site. Every load will need to make that journey.",
	"Abedju has commissions for three tombs. A yard that serves the first work site may be a long walk from the third. Leave a road between the work camps, the bricklayers and the land reserved for the dead.",
	"A messenger can reach Selima Oasis long before the soldiers he has come to summon. Keep enough people at home to run the settlement while a company is away in the service of the kingdom.",
	"The success of Abu will be measured in its inhabited streets. Set the workshops far enough from the houses for families to prosper, and close enough for their workers to find them.",
	"At Saqqara a stepped pyramid will rise above the roofs. Reserve its footprint and the road where the sledges will wait. The first deliveries must reach the masons without cutting the town off from its food.",
	"The crown keeps a close account of Serabit Khadim. The timing of a reply can matter as much as the size of a shipment. Open the mission ledger when the first request arrives.",
	"Meidum must support two stepped pyramids. Decide which work camp can spare a sledge before opening another building site. The city's families will still need food while both foremen are calling for workers.",
	"At Buhen the stone for an obelisk must be gathered before its base can be laid. The granite a cart has promised elsewhere is already spoken for. Set aside a yard for the royal commission.",
	"South Dahshur has been chosen for a bent pyramid. Its core and casing put different demands on the stone yards. Follow both supplies before sending workers to prepare the site.",
	"The smooth sides of North Dahshur's pyramid begin as ordinary loads of stone. Allow space for carts, returning crews and the limestone that will cover the finished core.",
	"The masons at Iunet want their brick deliveries on time. The treasury also has to support families, workshops and temples. Give the town a dependable income before committing every cart to the tomb.",
	"Three small mastabas have been commissioned at On. Their individual footprints are modest. Together, their bricks and labor will occupy a district for years.",
	"At Rostja a Sphinx will be carved from the rock beside the pyramids. Its crews need timber and skilled hands. Plan their approach alongside the far longer stone supply to the pyramid works.",
	"The sun temple at Bahariya Oasis needs its sandstone and its approach. Reserve the court, the corridor and the foretemple before new houses close off the ground.",
	"Djedu is to sustain a sanctuary to the sun. Keep the sandstone yard supplied as the settlement grows around it. The completed court must belong to a thriving city.",
	"An obelisk has been promised at Dunqul Oasis. Count the granite in the yards and the people still needed to raise it. A reservation on a distant cart cannot be lifted into place.",
	"At Dakhla Oasis, the roads serving the households must also support a royal commission. Store the granite first and leave the guilds a clear path to the obelisk.",
	"Families are returning to Thinis. Establish the streets, temples and civic services that will persuade them to remain. The city's standing in the kingdom depends on what those neighborhoods can sustain.",
	"Waset has land set aside for a sun temple and a pyramid. Their skilled workers draw from the same households. Plan for both commissions while the first streets are still short.",
	"Three obelisks are to stand at Kebet. Each stone must be paid for before the guilds can raise it. The order in which the granite is committed is yours.",
	"Menat Khufu's brickmakers and stone yards have separate commissions. Let the obelisks draw from the granite reserve while the brick-core pyramid takes shape nearby.",
	"A Sphinx and two pyramids will mark Itjtawy. Keep space for the carving crews as well as the long trains of brick. A well-placed guild can serve more than one site.",
	"The commission at Iken calls for a large obelisk. Accumulate its granite without starving the ordinary workshops of storage. Only then can the carpenters and masons begin.",
	"At Sawu the royal works include a mausoleum and a brick-core pyramid. Give the sandstone and brick deliveries separate places to unload before their roads meet at the guilds.",
	"Heh must raise a mausoleum while keeping its households supplied. The sandstone laid aside at the start is only the first part of the commission.",
	"At Bubastis two obelisks share the city's skyline. The court also expects a rich civic life. A zoo can serve thousands, provided its keepers receive both game meat and straw.",
	"The first courses at Khmun are brick. The finished pyramid will need limestone as well. Establish the second supply while workers are still building up the core.",
	"Sauty has received an order for three pyramids of the same size. Separate work sites make it easier to see which foreman is waiting for brick, which for casing stone, and which for labor.",
	"Byblos must provide for the kingdom beyond its own streets. Keep an eye on incoming requests as the granite accumulates for one large obelisk and two smaller ones.",
	"Baki's mausoleum and pyramids require different materials. Place their staging roads before committing the land around them. The storage yards will be busy long after the foundations are complete.",
	"At Rowarty the stonecutters and bricklayers both have work ahead. Arrange food production and trade so their households can remain settled through all three royal commissions.",
	"The great pyramid complex at Hetepsenusret needs room to reach the river. Reserve that ground before the city spreads. Its other monuments and the zoo must serve a city that can still feed itself."
]
